<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hispaniola</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Ayiti, Quisqueya</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hispaniola is the second-largest island of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caribbean">Caribbean</a>, in the Greater Antilles, shared today by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haiti">Haiti</a> in the west and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dominican-republic">Dominican Republic</a> in the east. Called Ayiti by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taino">Taíno</a>, it was reached by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Columbus</a> in 1492 and became the first base of Spanish colonisation in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/americas">Americas</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/santo-domingo">Santo Domingo</a>. Its western third became the French colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-domingue">Saint-Domingue</a> in 1697.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hispaniola.jpg" alt="Hispaniola" width="320" height="218" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Earth Science and Remote Sensing Unit, Lyndon B. Johnson Space Center · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:ISS027-E-17333_-_View_of_Dominican_Republic.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
